  "prompt_text": "The image is a schematic of a lead–iodide perovskite crystal operating in an acidic iodide electrolyte under illumination, apparently illustrating photocatalytic hydrogen production together with iodine redox chemistry. A pink-and-gray lattice labeled perovskite crystal is surrounded by an electrolyte identified as HI saturated with PbI₂. Blue wavy lines marked light strike the crystal and generate an electron–hole pair inside it.\n\nThe separated charges then move in opposite directions through the crystal: electrons are shown traveling toward the left surface, where nearby protons are reduced to H₂, while holes move toward the right surface. On the oxidative side, iodide-containing species near the surface, including I⁻ and a PbI₃⁻ complex, appear to participate in hole-driven oxidation that ultimately forms I₃⁻. Dashed arrows emphasize the paths of the electron and hole through the material.\n\nThe figure’s main message is that the perovskite acts as a light absorber and charge-separating semiconductor in solution, using photogenerated electrons for hydrogen evolution and photogenerated holes for iodide oxidation. The surrounding iodide/lead-iodide species seem to help stabilize the perovskite surface and mediate the redox process.",
